> oh ok, I used audacity (I use it on various computers without issues).
> I can try with other applications if you want.

For the primary testing, use arecord to eliminate unknown factors.
For example, 

	% arecord -fcd foo.wav

for 44.1kHz or

	% arecord -fdat foo.wav

for 48kHz.
If you get noises, try -Dplughw:0 option to skip dsnoop plugin.


> > /etc/asound.state is generated after you call "alsactl store" as
> > root.  This is done usually by alsasound init script.  You can do
> > "alsactl -f test.state store" as a normal user, too.
> 
> Ok, here is the file.

> [2 test.state <text/plain; us-ascii (7bit)>]
> state.Intel {
(snip)
> 	control.10 {
> 		comment.access 'read write'
> 		comment.type INTEGER
> 		comment.count 2
> 		comment.range '0 - 35'
> 		iface MIXER
> 		name 'Capture Volume'
> 		value.0 0
> 		value.1 0
> 	}

You didn't set this volume.  Raise "Capture" volume in alsamixer
(press [F4] to move to capture view mode).

> 	control.16 {
> 		comment.access 'read write'
> 		comment.type ENUMERATED
> 		comment.count 1
> 		comment.item.0 Mic
> 		comment.item.1 'Front Mic'
> 		comment.item.2 Line
> 		comment.item.3 CD
> 		iface MIXER
> 		name 'Input Source'
> 		value Mic
> 	}

So, the input comes from the microphone.
I'm not sure whether it's connected from front-mic or mic on your
device.


